The gold manifest treats all engine artifacts alike, but only game-surface ones need retained per-version history: language-semantics corpora want one current version plus a drift report


## Raised by Max, 2026-09-01

> "do we want to keep multiple versions of those tied to the openplanet version?
> unless it's to do with game classes cause they can change with game version
> updates"

**The instinct is right and it splits the manifest cleanly.** The 14 rows fall
into two groups with genuinely different needs.

## The distinction: does an OLD version's answer remain evidence?

**GAME SURFACE — retain per-version, because you DIFF them.**

| artifact | why |
|---|---|
| `tests/fixtures/typedb` | the type dump itself; changes every game update |
| `b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv` | names specific game enums (`Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice`) |
| `b053-bare-enum-values.tsv` | names specific game enum values (`Turbo`, `GlassSmash`) |
| `b052-scope-provenance.tsv` | which scopes existed; a surface question |

A member added or removed between versions is **the finding**, and you can only
see it by holding both. This is the deprecation/ABI story — GH #18, B094, and
B124's constness drift all live here. **typedb already implements exactly this**
(`typedb-status` / `diff` / `refresh` / `refresh-apply` / `typedb-guard`), so the
pattern exists and should be extended to the three enum-surface corpora rather
than invented.

**LANGUAGE SEMANTICS — one current version, plus the drift report.**

`b067`, `b071`, `b086`, both `b096` corpora, `b117`, `b119`, and all three
PRIMARY sites. These measure how the AngelScript compiler and Openplanet's module
system BEHAVE, not what the game exposes. When the rule changes you **replace**
the answer; the old one is not evidence about anything you would act on.

Keeping those live side-by-side costs review noise and creates a real hazard:
**someone cites the stale version.** This batch spent a night on exactly that
class of defect.

## The argument that decides it

**Git already gives you history for free.** The question is not "keep or lose the
old data" — every previous version of every corpus is in the log. The question is
whether to hold multiple versions **live, side by side, and comparable**, and
that is only worth its cost where something actually diffs them.

For a language rule nothing diffs them: the drift report says CHANGED, a human
reads why, and the new answer replaces the old. For game surface the diff IS the
product.

## Proposed mechanism

Add a **`volatility`** column to `docs/probes/MANIFEST.tsv`:

- `SURFACE` — retain per-version; regen adds a version rather than replacing;
  a diff between versions is a reportable finding.
- `SEMANTICS` — single current version; regen replaces in place; a change is
  reported as DRIFT for a human to read, and git holds the history.

The runner already refuses silent cross-version comparison (B129), so this only
changes what a regen WRITES, not what it compares.

## One middle case, worth naming rather than forcing

**Message-text corpora.** `b067`'s discriminator is literally two message
spellings, and `interface_impl.rs` records exact engine error strings. Those can
change on an engine upgrade with **no semantic change at all**.

They are `SEMANTICS` — you do not want a growing archive of wording — but they
need the version stamp to explain a diff, and a drift there should be readable as
"wording changed" rather than "behaviour changed". Say so in the `question`
column so the next reader is not alarmed by a cosmetic diff.

## Do the stamps first — this is blocked on real work, not on the design

The classification is cheap; the data is not currently in a state to act on it:

- **5 of 14 rows are `engine_version=UNKNOWN`** — `b052-scope-provenance`,
  `b117`, `b119`, `config.rs`, `server/mod.rs`.
- **`tests/fixtures/typedb` is stamped 1.29.5 while the live engine is 1.29.14.**
  The SURFACE artifact most in need of per-version history is itself a version
  behind.
- **B148**: `b067` and `b071` carry a stamp that looks copied from a sibling
  corpus, and they are the two orphans with no generator.

**Restamp and re-measure before adding a retention policy**, or the policy will
be applied to labels nobody has verified.


## AMENDMENT from B148's regeneration, 2026-09-01 — the split has a hybrid the two labels do not cover

lane-unused regenerated both orphan corpora at 1.29.14 and hit a case that
breaks the clean SURFACE/SEMANTICS division:

**`b067-class-position-scopes.tsv` is a HYBRID.** It measures a **language rule**
— can a class name qualify a type? — over an enumeration of **41 game class
names**. A game update removing `CGxLightBall` changes a row for a SURFACE
reason, inside an artifact classified SEMANTICS.

So the question the design has to answer is: **is `volatility` per-artifact, or
per-column?**

The same question is raised independently by B144's new `engine_messages`
column — the manifest's column set is already growing per-artifact, so the shape
"one label for the whole file" may be wrong for more than this one row.

## The middle case fired on the FIRST regeneration, exactly as predicted

`b071-enum-conversion.tsv`: **3 WORDING-CHANGED, 0 behaviour changes.** The
engine now names an assignment target as a reference — `to 'VirtualKey'` is today
`to 'VirtualKey&'`.

That is B151's message-text case occurring in the wild on its first run, and the
lane's `INSTRUMENT-BROKEN`-first ordering handled it correctly. It confirms the
mechanism is needed, and it also shows the mechanism is not sufficient for the
b067 hybrid above.

**And it was not read as confirming the preamble.** The competing explanation —
that the reconstructed fixture differed from the original, since the `fixture`
column is abbreviated rather than literal source — was tested and ruled out by
probing init / assign / argument shapes before any conclusion was drawn.

## The other regeneration result, for the record

**`b067`: byte-identical at 1.29.14. 63/63 SAME, 22/22 controls passed.** So the
`1.29.5 / 1234ad9e` stamp was mislabelled and **nothing drawn from those 63 rows
is wrong.** A second result rides along free: the committed corpus measured its
41 SAMPLE rows in one B068-unsafe batch, and re-measured one-per-compilation-unit
they are unchanged — so that batch was not silently corrupt.

## Still true, and still the precondition

5 of the manifest's rows carry `engine_version=UNKNOWN`, and
`tests/fixtures/typedb` — the SURFACE artifact most in need of per-version
history — remains stamped 1.29.5 while the live engine is 1.29.14. **Restamp
before applying a retention policy to labels nobody has verified.**